The Mercedes-Benz CLS 350 CDI was part of the second generation (C218), produced from 2011 to 2018. The most recent version would be from the later years of this generation, potentially including the facelift from 2014 onwards, where it was sometimes referred to as CLS 350 BlueTEC. The specifications are largely consistent for the 350 CDI/BlueTEC variant within the C218 generation, with some variations in power output (258 PS vs 265 PS) and transmission (7G-Tronic Plus vs 9G-Tronic on later BlueTEC models). I will use data primarily from the 2014-2018 C218 CLS 350 CDI/BlueTEC, noting variations where relevant.

- **price\_usd**: The CLS 350 CDI is from an older generation (C218, 2011-2018). Used prices are significantly lower than the average new car price. Used examples from 2011-2013 are listed around £6,450 - £22,990 in the UK (~$8,200 - $29,300 USD). A 2013 model is listed at €22,750 (~$24,600 USD). This is well below the market average of ~$52,000. Score: +0.80.
- **total\_cost\_estimate\_5yr\_usd**: No specific 5-year cost estimate found. As an older diesel luxury car, maintenance and potential repairs could be higher than average, but depreciation is lower than a new car. Default to 0.00.
- **insurance\_usd\_yr**: UK Insurance Group is around 36-50 for the C218 CLS range. The 350 CDI is likely in the higher end of this range. This is high, suggesting higher than average insurance costs. Score: -0.75.
- **maint\_usd\_yr**: No specific maintenance cost found. Mercedes-Benz maintenance is generally higher than average, and older diesel engines can have specific maintenance needs (e.g., OM642 oil cooler leaks). Default to 0.00.
- **repair\_risk\_score**: The OM642 engine is considered reasonably reliable if properly serviced, but common issues include oil cooler leaks and injector seal leaks. The 7G-Tronic transmission can have valve body or electronic plate issues around 80k miles. Airmatic suspension, if equipped, can be expensive to maintain. Overall, reliability is considered average for its age if well-maintained, but potential costly repairs exist. Score: 0.00.
- **hp**: The CLS 350 CDI BlueEFFICIENCY (2011-2014) has 265 PS (261 hp). The CLS 350 BlueTEC (2014-2017) has 258 PS (255 hp). Using the range of 255-261 hp. This is above the market average of ~200 hp. Score: 0.30.
- **torque**: The CLS 350 CDI/BlueTEC has 620 Nm (457 lb-ft). This is well above the average of ~190 lb-ft. Score: 0.75.
- **cylinder\_count**: The CLS 350 CDI/BlueTEC has a V6 engine. A V6 is around the average for performance-oriented cars, but above the general market average which includes many 4-cylinders. Score: 0.25.
- **engine\_displacement**: The CLS 350 CDI/BlueTEC has a 3.0L (2987 cm3) engine. This is above the general market average. Score: 0.25.
- **accel\_0\_60\_sec**: 0-100 km/h is 6.2 seconds for the 265 hp version and 6.5-6.7 seconds for the 258 hp version. This is approximately 5.8-6.3 seconds 0-60 mph. This is significantly faster than the market average of ~7.5 seconds. Score: 0.50.
- **top\_speed**: The top speed is 245-250 km/h (152-155 mph). This is around the average of 150 mph. Score: 0.00.
- **drive\_type**: The CLS 350 CDI/BlueTEC was available in both Rear-Wheel Drive (RWD) and 4MATIC (All-Wheel Drive). RWD is rated +1.00, AWD is 0.00. Since both were available, average the score. Score: 0.50.
